(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 19-year-old man develops an intensely pruritic vesicular eruption in linear streaks two days after clearing brush. What immunological mechanism underlies allergic contact dermatitis, and what is its most common cause in the United States?","choices":["A type III immune complex reaction","Delayed type IV reaction, most often to urushiol","Immediate type I reaction to urushiol","Direct barrier disruption, no immune role","Delayed type IV reaction, most often to nickel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Allergic contact dermatitis is an immunologic cell-mediated, delayed, type IV hypersensitivity reaction. Urushiol sap of Toxicodendron species, found in poison ivy, poison sumac, and poison oak, is the most common cause, and roughly 50 to 75 percent of people in the United States are allergic to these plants. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman with dermatitis herpetiformis achieves rapid relief on dapsone and asks whether that is the whole treatment. What is the most important additional element of management?","choices":["Lifelong systemic corticosteroids","A gluten-free diet for six weeks","A strict lifelong gluten-free diet","High-potency topical steroids alone","Indefinite dapsone monotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dapsone provides rapid symptom relief but the long-term management is a strict gluten-free diet, because the disease is driven by immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase in patients consuming dietary gluten. All patients should also be screened for celiac disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has fifteen discrete, smooth, firm, flesh-coloured dome-shaped papules averaging 4 mm on the trunk and arms, each with a central dimple. His parents ask about treatment. What is a reasonable approach?","choices":["Curettage of every lesion at the first visit","Oral acyclovir","Observation; procedures may scar or pigment","Topical mupirocin to each lesion","Salicylic acid 40 percent to each lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Molluscum contagiosum is a benign poxvirus infection, and observation is appropriate for many patients because procedures may blister, pigment, or scar. Approved options such as berdazimer gel and cantharidin exist when treatment is chosen. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 136"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with cellulitis of the leg has an area that is tense, cyanotic, and bronzed with blanching. What is the significance of this finding?","choices":["Increase the dose and observe","A drug reaction to the antibiotic","Devitalised tissue; antibiotics cannot reach it","Deep vein thrombosis; anticoagulate","The expected look of resolving cellulitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Devitalised tissue that is tense, cyanotic, necrotic, bronzed, and blanched will not be perfused, so antibiotics will not get to the site. Surgical debridement is required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 14-year-old boy with neurofibromatosis type 1 has a large, diffuse soft tissue mass running along a nerve in the thigh. What is a plexiform neurofibroma, and what is the concern with it?","choices":["A discrete nerve sheath tumour at the surface","A pigmented macule growing with the child","Freckles under 5 mm in body folds","A large tumour in nerve covering that may invade","A tumour confined to brain and spinal cord"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plexiform neurofibromas are tumours in the tissue covering nerves, occurring anywhere except the brain and spinal cord. They are large and extensive and may be locally invasive, so clinical evaluation is needed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with no symptoms attends for a routine health check, while a 55-year-old woman attends the same clinic with a specific complaint requiring evaluation. How does screening testing differ in purpose from diagnostic testing?","choices":["Screening confirms a diagnosis; diagnosis seeks occult disease","Diagnostic testing replaces the examination","They are interchangeable","Screening seeks occult disease; diagnosis reduces uncertainty","Screening is only for emergency medicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Screening testing identifies the likelihood of occult disease. Diagnostic testing complements the history and physical examination, reduces uncertainty about diagnosis or prognosis, and helps decide management. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl with red hair has multiple freckles across the nose and cheeks, and her mother asks what can be done about them. What is the cornerstone of management for ephelides?","choices":["No management is possible","Systemic depigmenting therapy","Sun protection with patient education","Surgical excision of each macule","Cryotherapy to each lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sun protection is the cornerstone of management for ephelides, with proper patient education and counselling described as key. Topical depigmenting agents such as hydroquinone, retinoids, alpha-hydroxy acids, and botanicals may be used, and intense pulsed light is an option. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has had erythematous scaly patches and plaques on the trunk for four years, several larger than 5 cm, that have been treated as psoriasis and eczema without lasting response. Pruritus is prominent. What features discriminate this from the inflammatory conditions it resembles?","choices":["Scale","Chronicity, treatment resistance, odd distribution","Truncal involvement","Pruritus","A positive potassium hydroxide preparation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mycosis fungoides may resemble psoriasis, eczema, or tinea, and the discriminators are chronicity, treatment resistance, and large or oddly distributed lesions. Early morphology is localised or generalised erythematous patches or scaly plaques, usually on the trunk, frequently greater than 5 cm. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man returns from a hotel stay with several itchy welts on exposed skin. Examination shows wheals and papules with a haemorrhagic punctum, arranged in rows of three. He did not feel the bites occur. What organism is responsible, and what is the classic descriptive term?","choices":["Cimex lectularius; breakfast, lunch, and dinner","Cercariae; urticaria after water exposure","Pediculus humanus corporis; linear excoriations","Sarcoptes scabiei; the delta-wing jet","Tunga penetrans; translucent foot nodules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bedbugs are nocturnal feeders that hide in cracks and crevices of headboards, picture frames, and behind loose wallpaper. Bites are painless, multiple, and grouped in a linear fashion, and a row of three bites is known as breakfast, lunch, and dinner. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with alcohol use disorder develops severe leg pain and rapidly progressive skin changes after a minor injury. Which set of risk factors is associated with necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["Obesity, poor hygiene, occlusive clothing, humidity","Wet work and chemical exposure","Manicure, ingrown nail, and nail biting","Trauma, burns, surgery, immunosuppression, alcoholism","Lymphatic impairment, athlete's foot, obesity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those are the listed risk factors, and the condition is more common in males than females. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man who has worked outdoors for forty years is examined. Several pink papules 0.3 to 0.5 cm are visible on the scalp, and running a hand across the skin reveals further lesions that could not be seen at all. What is the characteristic tactile finding?","choices":["Pearly with telangiectasias","A stuck-on quality","Velvety texture","Boggy and fluctuant","Sandpaper texture"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses are small 0.2 to 0.6 cm flesh-coloured, pink, or slightly hyperpigmented papules with a characteristic sandpaper texture on palpation, and a lesion may be more apparent by touch than by sight. The skin must therefore be palpated, not only inspected. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman is treated for scabies and asks what she should do with her sheets and clothing. What environmental measure is specified for bedding and clothing?","choices":["Wash, or bag for 14 days; 60 degrees Celsius kills mites","No environmental measures needed","Wash in cold water","Bag for 24 hours","Discard all bedding and clothing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bedding and clothing should be washed or set aside for 14 days in a plastic bag in a warm area, and high heat at 60 degrees Celsius is needed to kill mites. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman presents with chronic bilateral lower-leg swelling, pruritus, and skin discoloration. Examination demonstrates poorly demarcated erythematous plaques, pitting edema, and brown hyperpigmentation around both medial ankles. Pedal pulses are diminished. What is the most appropriate next step before prescribing compression therapy?","choices":["Begin oral cephalexin","Perform an ankle-brachial index","Order patch testing","Perform a skin biopsy","Obtain a bacterial wound culture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Compression is the cornerstone of management only once adequate arterial circulation has been established. An ankle-brachial index or toe pressure should be obtained when peripheral arterial disease is suspected — as the diminished pulses here suggest — before initiating substantial compression. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 17-year-old boy starting topical tretinoin and benzoyl peroxide for acne asks how to use them. What education should be given?","choices":["Apply them together for better penetration","Apply both twice daily; clear in a week","Use only one of the two","Separate them by at least three hours","Apply together, washing vigorously between"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Topical tretinoin and benzoyl peroxide should not be applied together because of the risk of skin irritation, and at least three hours should separate them. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has folliculitis that has recurred repeatedly despite topical treatment, and cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus each time. What additional measure addresses the likely reservoir?","choices":["An antibacterial soap alone","Oral ciprofloxacin","Mupirocin to the nares for the carrier state","A potassium hydroxide mount of a hair","A longer course of the same cream"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent or recalcitrant folliculitis raises the Staphylococcus aureus carrier state, and mupirocin ointment is used for it. Nasal swabs of the patient and family members are taken to evaluate carriage. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has a healing surgical wound and asks why the scar keeps getting stronger over months. What accounts for the improving tensile strength of a maturing scar?","choices":["Strength is fixed at the time of closure","Continued fibroblast proliferation","New elastin deposition","Vascular ingrowth","Progressive cross-linking of collagen fibres"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The four phases are hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and remodeling. As the scar matures its tensile strength improves because of progressive cross-linking of collagen fibres. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 3-month-old infant with blistering at sites of minor friction is being investigated to establish where in the skin the split is occurring. What diagnostic test is considered the gold standard for establishing the level of cleavage in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation of blister fluid","Direct immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin A deposits","Immunofluorescence antigen mapping","Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy","Genetic testing alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy is the gold standard for determining the cleavage plane. Immunofluorescence antigen mapping localises missing or reduced proteins, and genetic testing is confirmatory and guides prognosis and family counselling. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with diabetes has three thickened, discoloured, crumbling toenails with distal onycholysis. He asks for oral terbinafine. What must be done first?","choices":["Remove the nails surgically first","Start terbinafine empirically","Obtain a Wood's lamp examination","Start topical antifungal therapy","Confirm the fungus; many dystrophic nails are not"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Fungus must be confirmed before oral therapy, since many dystrophic nails are not fungal. Testing options include potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid-Schiff stain of nail clippings, fungal culture, or polymerase chain reaction, sampling the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 19-year-old presents in summer with numerous well-defined hypopigmented macules over the upper back and shoulders that do not tan. Scraping the surface reveals fine scale. What does potassium hydroxide microscopy show?","choices":["Long septate branching hyphae","Budding yeast with pseudohyphae","No organisms; biopsy is needed","Short hyphae with yeast clusters","Mites, eggs, and fecal pellets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Potassium hydroxide microscopy in pityriasis versicolor shows short hyphae with clusters of yeast, classically described as spaghetti and meatballs. The condition is caused by overgrowth of lipid-dependent Malassezia species that normally inhabit the skin. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman presents with right-sided facial weakness, painful vesicles in the right ear canal, and vertigo. What is the diagnosis, and what treatment is added beyond antivirals?","choices":["Ramsay Hunt syndrome; add a systemic corticosteroid","Ramsay Hunt syndrome; an antiviral alone","Otitis externa; antibiotic drops","Bell palsy; a corticosteroid alone","Herpes zoster ophthalmicus; ophthalmology referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ramsay Hunt syndrome, or herpes zoster oticus, is peripheral facial palsy with painful vesicles of the ear canal, auricle, or oropharynx, sometimes with hearing loss, tinnitus, or vertigo. Antiviral therapy plus a systemic corticosteroid is started early when not contraindicated. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 5-month-old girl has a large hemangioma of the face lying close to the eye and the corner of the mouth. What complications should be considered?","choices":["Malignant transformation to angiosarcoma","Compression of vital structures and blocked vision","Progressive darkening throughout life","No complications occur","Systemic hypertension from shunting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemangiomas may compress vital structures, block vision, interfere with feeding or respiration, obstruct the external auditory canal, and occur at extracutaneous sites. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl develops an abrupt eruption of numerous small raindrop-shaped scaly papules and plaques over the trunk and proximal limbs. Her mother reports she was treated for a sore throat about two and a half weeks ago. She is otherwise well. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral methotrexate","Topical mupirocin to the affected areas","Oral acitretin","Reassurance, with phototherapy or topical steroids only if needed","Systemic corticosteroids tapered over four weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Guttate psoriasis presents as raindrop-shaped erythematous papules and plaques, typically in children 2 to 3 weeks following a streptococcal infection or upper respiratory infection. No treatment is needed, though phototherapy and topical steroids may be used. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 164"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man develops severe nocturnal itching six weeks after his partner was treated for scabies. How is scabies typically transmitted, and how long after infestation does pruritus appear?","choices":["Brief casual contact; itch within 24 hours","Contaminated water; itch within hours","Close contact for 15 to 20 minutes; itch at 4 to 6 weeks","Head-to-head contact; itch at 4 to 6 weeks","Airborne spread; itch after 1 week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Scabies is transmitted by close physical contact for 15 to 20 minutes and may also be acquired from the bedding or underclothing of an infested individual. Pruritus appears 4 to 6 weeks after initial infestation, and many patients do not develop symptoms for up to 3 months. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with tender pretibial nodules undergoes a deep incisional biopsy. What finding confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Full-thickness epidermal necrosis","Septal panniculitis without vasculitis, with Miescher's granulomas","Palisading granulomas with central necrobiosis and mucin","A dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate with no organisms","Lobular panniculitis with vasculitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy shows septal panniculitis without vasculitis and Miescher's granulomas. A deep incisional biopsy is needed because the pathology lies in the subcutaneous fat. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man is referred with a red, warm, tender left lower leg. He has bilateral chronic ankle swelling and brown discoloration of both gaiter regions, but the left leg has become acutely more swollen and painful over two days. He is afebrile. What consideration should most urgently shape the evaluation?","choices":["Start compression immediately","Bilateral change makes cellulitis likely","Brown discoloration means infection","No fever excludes a vascular event","Acute unilateral swelling suggests venous thrombosis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The differential for stasis dermatitis explicitly flags that acute unilateral swelling or pain should raise concern for deep venous thrombosis. Chronic bilateral changes with a new unilateral acute change is precisely that pattern. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy is brought in with fever and a rash. Examination shows non-blanching deposits of blood in the skin, several measuring 6 mm across. What is the most appropriate interpretation of this finding?","choices":["Ecchymosis from minor trauma","A wheal from dermal fluid","Purpura, an emergency until proven otherwise","Petechiae, an emergency until proven otherwise","Purpura, benign and observable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Purpura is a deposit of blood 4 mm or greater, and the lecture flags it explicitly as a medical emergency until proven otherwise. Combined with fever in a child this demands immediate evaluation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 9-month-old boy is brought in for a diaper rash of one week. Examination shows beefy erythema involving the inguinal folds, with peripheral scale and several small papules and pustules on the thighs a short distance beyond the main area of redness. He completed a course of amoxicillin two weeks ago. What finding most strongly indicates the infectious complication rather than simple irritant disease?","choices":["Erythema in the diaper area","The one-week duration","Fold involvement with satellite lesions","Scaling at the edge","The recent amoxicillin course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Whether the skin folds are involved is the finding that distinguishes irritant from candidal disease. Irritant diaper dermatitis affects the convex surfaces and generally spares the inguinal folds because they are less exposed to urine and feces; candidal disease produces beefy erythema in the folds with satellite papules and pustules. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man presents with a painful swelling in the natal cleft, and the clinician reviews what predisposed him to it. Which set of risk factors is associated with pilonidal disease?","choices":["Fair skin, sun exposure, age over 40","Obesity, sedentary life, dense natal cleft hair","Venous insufficiency and lymphoedema","Prematurity and low birth weight","Female sex, pregnancy, oestrogen excess"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those are the listed risk factors, and the male to female ratio is 3 to 1. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with plaque psoriasis reports painful, stiff finger joints. He describes more than thirty minutes of morning stiffness that improves as he uses his hands during the day. Examination shows diffuse swelling of the entire second and third digits of the right hand. What additional finding is most characteristic of the associated condition?","choices":["A positive rheumatoid factor","Human leukocyte antigen Cw6","Stiffness worsening with activity","Symmetric small joints, sparing distal","Human leukocyte antigen B27"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Psoriatic arthritis is associated with human leukocyte antigen B27. The presentation described — psoriasis with joint pains frequently of the hands, stiffness lasting more than thirty minutes that is relieved with activity, and sausage digits — is the classic picture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has maceration and erythematous erosions between the third and fourth toes with associated fissuring. What form of tinea pedis is this, and is it the commonest?","choices":["Interdigital tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Interdigital tinea pedis, which is the least common form","Hyperkeratotic tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Vesiculobullous tinea pedis, which is the most common form","Candidal intertrigo of the toe webs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Interdigital tinea pedis is the most common form, caused by Trichophyton species, producing maceration and erythematous erosions or scales between the toes, especially in the third and fourth interspaces, with associated fissures. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 36-year-old pregnant woman and her 7-year-old son both develop erythema migrans after a camping trip. What is first-line oral antibiotic therapy for Lyme disease, and what alternative applies to children and pregnant women with early disease?","choices":["Topical permethrin to the bite","Doxycycline, with amoxicillin in children and pregnancy","Amoxicillin, with doxycycline for failure","No antibiotics for stage 1","Intravenous ceftriaxone for all stages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are indicated in all stages. Doxycycline is first line, with amoxicillin as an alternative first-line agent for early disease including erythema migrans, particularly in children and pregnant women. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with erythema multiforme triggered by recurrent herpes simplex virus has had four episodes in twelve months. What management addresses the recurrences?","choices":["Suppressive oral acyclovir or valacyclovir","Continuous topical corticosteroids","Continuous oral antihistamines","Oral prednisone at each episode","Avoid anti-inflammatories and sulfonamides"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral acyclovir or valacyclovir is used when the eruption is triggered by herpes simplex virus, and a prior episode is itself a risk factor for recurrence. Suppressing the trigger is what prevents the next episode. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has a hard, conical keratotic projection on the dorsum of his hand. What is the key principle in evaluating this lesion?","choices":["A conical shape means benign","Send the horn; leave the base alone","Cryotherapy without histology","Painless means it can be reassured","Diagnose the lesion at the base"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A cutaneous horn arises from the surface of another lesion, which may be benign or malignant — actinic keratosis, warts, and squamous cell carcinoma among them. There is often no clinical feature to distinguish them, so the underlying lesion must be diagnosed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has been applying a topical corticosteroid to the same area of eyelid skin for several months. What adverse effects should be anticipated?","choices":["Permanent loss of hair follicles at the treated site","Atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, and hypopigmentation","Nephrotoxicity with prolonged application","Photosensitivity, dryness, and irritation","Hyperpigmentation, hypertrichosis, and thickening of the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prolonged use of topical corticosteroids may cause atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, and hypopigmentation. This is why potency is selected according to site and severity and why the usual course is twice daily for two weeks. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has a circumscribed light brown patch on the trunk resembling a café au lait spot, within which several darker pigmented macules and papules are visible. What is this lesion and how is it managed?","choices":["A dysplastic naevus","Nevus spilus; excise immediately","A cafe au lait macule","A blue nevus","Nevus spilus; observe with sun protection"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Nevus spilus, the spotted nevus, is a variant of congenital naevus present at birth or in the first years of life, most commonly on the trunk and extremities. Darkly pigmented macules or papules sit within a background that is circumscribed and similar in appearance to a café au lait spot, and it rarely progresses to melanoma. Management is observation, periodic evaluation, and sun protection. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man in equatorial Africa presents with rapidly progressive violaceous nodules on both legs. He is not known to have human immunodeficiency virus infection and is not taking immunosuppressive therapy. Which clinical form of Kaposi sarcoma is described as often aggressive and potentially rapidly fatal in young Black men in equatorial Africa?","choices":["The classic form","The endemic form","The epidemic form","The iatrogenic form","The nodular form"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The endemic form is often aggressive disease in young Black men in equatorial Africa and can be rapidly fatal, in contrast to the classic form in older men, which follows a chronic course and is rarely fatal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl is evaluated for a lesion on the finger pad that appeared rapidly after she injured the finger and bleeds easily when bumped. Examination shows a bright red, friable, pedunculated papule. What is the diagnosis, and what is notable about its name?","choices":["Pyogenic granuloma, neither infectious nor granulomatous","Pyogenic granuloma, a granulomatous infection","Dermatofibroma","Cherry angioma","Amelanotic melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pyogenic granuloma is an acquired vascular lesion that is misnamed, being neither infectious nor granulomatous. It is a benign vascular tumour common in children, young adults, and pregnancy, arising as a response to injury or hormonal factors, most often on the head, neck, and fingers. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 29-year-old hairdresser reports recurrent episodes of intensely itchy blisters on her hands. Examination shows multiple grouped 1 to 2 mm vesicles on the lateral aspects of the fingers and the adjacent interdigital surfaces, some coalescing, on a poorly defined pink base with surrounding dryness. She reports previous episodes that persisted for several weeks, dried up, and then peeled. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Irritant contact dermatitis","Dyshidrotic eczema","Atopic dermatitis of the hands","Nummular eczema","Allergic contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dyshidrotic eczema is a chronic, relapsing eruption of intensely pruritic vesicles on the hands and feet, classically described as tapioca vesicles. They may coalesce into bullae, persist for several weeks, then desiccate and resolve with desquamation — the exact course she describes. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman has a tender fluctuant abscess of the buttock that has not drained spontaneously. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Observation until the lesion drains on its own","Oral antibiotics selected by culture of the intact skin surface","Squeezing the lesion to express its contents","Warm soaks and broad-spectrum antibiotics alone","Surgical incision and drainage"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. If the lesion does not spontaneously drain, surgical incision and drainage is required. If it drains spontaneously, warm soaks and broad-spectrum antibiotics with consideration of methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus are used, adjusted by culture. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 27-year-old pregnant woman has several lesions on the upper trunk consisting of a central arteriole with radiating capillaries. What is the mechanism, and what should be asked in the history?","choices":["Capillary proliferation with age; no history needed","Dilated existing vessels; ask about oestrogen and alcohol","A vascular tumour after minor trauma","Blood extravasated into the dermis","Dilated dermal capillaries present from birth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nevus araneus involves no vascular proliferation and is due to dilation of pre-existing vessels. Oestrogen excess states may be the cause, and lesions resolve after delivery or cessation of hormonal contraception. History should cover pregnancies, hormone use, alcohol, and high-risk medications for liver damage. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman reports gradual hair thinning over several years. Examination shows diffuse thinning across the central and parietal scalp with preservation of the frontal hairline. There are no bald patches, no scaling, no scarring, and no exclamation point hairs. Several of her male relatives began balding in their twenties. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Female-pattern hair loss","Lichen planopilaris","Tinea capitis","Male-pattern hair loss","Alopecia areata"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Female-pattern hair loss produces diffuse thinning of the central and parietal scalp without significant change to the frontal hairline. There is an increased frequency of balding among first-degree male relatives, which the family history here supplies. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 146"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy develops multiple erythematous papules arranged in linear streaks after handling a caterpillar. What is the term for the aggregate of medical effects caused by caterpillars, moths, and butterflies, and what is a specific step in management?","choices":["Tungiasis; excision","Erucism; systemic corticosteroids only","Arachnidism; antivenom","Cercarial dermatitis; oatmeal baths","Lepidopterism; strip the hairs off"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Lepidopterism is the aggregate of medical effects caused by caterpillars, moths, and butterflies. Gypsy moth caterpillar contact causes erucism, a pruritic dermatitis with erythematous papules in linear streaks. Management is symptomatic, and the hairs are removed by stripping. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has several lesions around the nose and mouth. Examination shows golden-yellow adherent crusts over shallow erosions, with palpable regional lymphadenopathy. He is afebrile and there are only four lesions. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical mupirocin, removing crusts first","Oral penicillin V","Oral cephalexin","Oral trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Topical mupirocin over the intact crusts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical therapy is used for a single or limited number of non-bullous lesions, and mupirocin ointment is adequate for most cases of impetigo and as effective as oral therapy with fewer side effects. Crusts should be removed before applying it. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with obesity, type 2 diabetes, and polycystic ovary syndrome has velvety hyperpigmented plaques of the neck and axillae. What is the primary management?","choices":["Topical retinoids","Reassurance with no intervention","Topical corticosteroids","Weight loss and glycaemic control","Laser therapy or dermabrasion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Primary management is treating the underlying cause through weight loss and glycaemic control, discontinuing any offending drug. Metformin reduces insulin resistance and may improve the skin findings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man develops an exaggerated sunburn-like reaction on sun-exposed skin within hours of starting a new medication. The reaction is dose-dependent and occurred on the very first exposure. What mechanism is responsible?","choices":["Phototoxicity, non-immunologic and dose-dependent","Phytophotodermatitis from plant contact","Phototoxicity, immunologic and dose-independent","Polymorphous light eruption","Photoallergy, immunologic and needing sensitisation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Phototoxicity is non-immunologic and dose-dependent — the drug absorbs ultraviolet energy, generating reactive oxygen species and direct cellular damage that resembles an exaggerated sunburn. Photopatch testing is the gold standard for photoallergy, the other mechanism. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has culture-confirmed tinea capitis and the species result will determine which drug he receives. Which oral agent is generally favoured for Trichophyton infection, and which for Microsporum?","choices":["Griseofulvin for Trichophyton and terbinafine for Microsporum","Terbinafine for Trichophyton and griseofulvin for Microsporum","Terbinafine for both organisms","Fluconazole for both organisms","Topical ketoconazole for both organisms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Terbinafine is generally favoured for Trichophyton infection and griseofulvin is often favoured for Microsporum infection, which is why identifying the organism by culture matters before prolonged systemic therapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has several tense, clear-to-cloudy blisters on the trunk arising on intact skin. Some have ruptured, leaving shallow moist erosions ringed by a collarette of scale. There is no lymphadenopathy. What organism and mechanism are responsible?","choices":["Staphylococcus or Streptococcus, via a skin break","Autoantibody against the basement membrane","Staphylococcus aureus alone, via epidermolytic toxin","Streptococcus pyogenes, by dermal invasion","Corynebacterium in the stratum corneum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bullous impetigo is caused exclusively by Staphylococcus aureus, which releases epidermolytic toxins producing epidermal splitting. Bullae occur on intact skin, rupture to leave collarettes, and lymphadenopathy is uncommon — in contrast to non-bullous disease, where it is common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man living in a shelter has linear excoriations of the back and waist, while a 6-year-old girl in the same clinic has an itchy scalp. How does the presentation of pediculosis corporis differ from that of pediculosis capitis?","choices":["Burrows in the finger webs","Spread by head-to-head contact","Scalp involvement with lymphadenopathy","Linear excoriations of the back, neck, and waist","It is always asymptomatic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Body lice produce pruritus with linear excoriations primarily on the back, neck, shoulders, and waist, and post-inflammatory pigmentation in chronic cases. Head lice affect the scalp with pruritus, low-grade fever, regional lymphadenopathy, and irritability. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman asks about treatment for photoaging. What topical agent is approved for this indication, and what should she be told to expect?","choices":["Calcipotriene","Tretinoin, with results in two weeks and no irritation","Tretinoin, with initial irritation over 6 to 12 months","Topical corticosteroids nightly","Hydroquinone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tretinoin 0.025 to 0.1 percent is the only approved topical agent for photoaging. It stimulates collagen synthesis, inhibits matrix metalloproteinase activity, and reduces fine lines and dyspigmentation; therapy is started low and titrated, with initial retinoid dermatitis expected. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 22-year-old bartender develops streaky, bizarrely shaped erythema and blistering on the forearms two days after an outdoor shift squeezing limes. The affected areas later become hyperpigmented. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Phytophotodermatitis","Polymorphous light eruption","Allergic contact dermatitis to citrus","Drug-induced phototoxicity","Ordinary sunburn"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Phytophotodermatitis follows contact with furanocoumarins, also called psoralens, found in limes, celery, parsley, and related plants, followed by ultraviolet exposure. The streaky shapes trace where the juice ran, and postinflammatory hyperpigmentation follows. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A student proposes a rare diagnosis to explain a very typical presentation in a 30-year-old woman. What does the principle 'place your bets on uncommon manifestations of common conditions rather than common manifestations of uncommon conditions' mean in practice?","choices":["A typical presentation of a rare disease is likelier","An atypical presentation of a common disease is likelier","Rare diagnoses should never be considered","Assume the most serious diagnosis first","All diagnoses are equally likely until tested"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Common things occur commonly — when you hear hoof beats think of horses, not zebras. An unusual presentation of a common condition is more probable than a textbook presentation of something rare. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has multiple actinic keratoses scattered across a bald scalp, and the clinician explains that treating only the individual visible lesions may be insufficient. What concept does this reflect?","choices":["Koebner phenomenon","Paraneoplastic change","Photoallergy","Field cancerisation","Pathergy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses represent a field cancerisation process, meaning the ultraviolet-induced mutations extend through the surrounding skin rather than being confined to the visible lesions. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man still has burning, stabbing pain four months after a thoracic zoster eruption cleared. How is postherpetic neuralgia defined, and what are the first-line treatments?","choices":["Pain beyond 90 days; long-term opioids","Pain beyond 7 days; antivirals","Pain beyond 30 days; corticosteroids","Pain beyond 90 days; gabapentin or a tricyclic","Any acute pain; acetaminophen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pain persisting 90 days or more after rash onset is the commonly used definition. First-line treatment is gabapentin or pregabalin, an appropriate tricyclic antidepressant, or topical lidocaine, with a capsaicin patch as a further option. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has a classic presentation of erysipelas with a sharply demarcated raised plaque of the shin and a fever of 39 degrees Celsius. What laboratory and imaging approach is appropriate?","choices":["Clinical; cultures and imaging add little","Skin biopsy in every patient","Blood and tissue cultures in every patient","Magnetic resonance imaging for depth","No laboratory testing is ever useful"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Erysipelas is a clinical diagnosis in a classic presentation. Leukocytosis and raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are common, but blood and tissue cultures are not cost effective because of extremely low organism yield, and imaging studies are of low yield and not indicated. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 15-year-old presents with molluscum lesions confined to the genital area. What is the appropriate approach?","choices":["Assess for other sexually transmitted infections","Assume abuse from the location alone","Refer for excision of all lesions","Reassure that it is never sexually transmitted","Give acyclovir and screen only for herpes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Genital lesions in adolescents and adults may be sexually transmitted and assessment for other infections is appropriate. In a child, genital lesions require context-sensitive assessment, and location alone does not prove abuse. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 6-week-old infant has a bright red papule on the shoulder, and the clinician classifies the lesion by the depth of vessel involvement. What are the three types of infantile hemangioma, and which is the most common?","choices":["Superficial, mixed, and deep; superficial commonest","Capillary, cavernous, arteriovenous","Proliferative, involuting, involuted","Congenital, infantile, acquired","Superficial, mixed, and deep; deep commonest"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The superficial type is the most common, with dilated vessels in the dermis surrounded by proliferating endothelial cells, presenting as a bright red papule, plaque, or nodule and previously called a strawberry hemangioma. The deep type is the least common. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has a new pigmented longitudinal band in the nail of one thumb that has widened over six months and is broader proximally than distally. Pigment extends onto the proximal nail fold. What is the concern, and what is the sign at the nail fold called?","choices":["Nail unit melanoma; Hutchinson sign","Subungual haematoma","Benign longitudinal melanonychia","Onychomycosis","Glomus tumour"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. New or evolving longitudinal melanonychia in one digit with increasing width, irregular colour or spacing, and proximal widening or a triangular shape raises nail unit melanoma. Pigment on the periungual skin is Hutchinson sign, and the thumb and great toe are high-yield sites. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman presents with an intensely itchy eruption on both wrists and ankles that has been present for two months. Examination shows small, flat-topped, shiny violaceous papules, some coalescing into plaques, over the volar wrists and ankles. Fine white lines are visible across the surface of the plaques. She also has lacy white streaks on the buccal mucosa. What are the surface lines called?","choices":["Civatte bodies","Koebner phenomenon","Wickham striae","Collarette of scale","Auspitz sign"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fine white lines visible on the surface of lichen planus plaques are known as Wickham striae. Oral lichen planus can occur with or without cutaneous disease and often presents with erosive lesions or Wickham striae, as it does here. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a chronic plaque on the ankle showing thickening and hardening of the skin with exaggerated surface markings, and adjacent skin showing accumulation of loose grey-white cornified fragments. What are these two secondary changes called?","choices":["Erosion and ulceration, respectively","Scaling and lichenification, respectively","Lichenification and scaling, respectively","Scar and fissure, respectively","Excoriation and crust, respectively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lichenification is thickening and hardening of the skin, and scaling is accumulation of loose or adherent cornified fragments of the epidermis, typically grey or white. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman presents on day four of a dermatomal vesicular eruption with new lesions still appearing. Within what window should antiviral therapy ideally be started, and when should it still be given beyond that window?","choices":["Only once postherpetic neuralgia develops","Within 7 days, with no exceptions","Within 72 hours, and later if lesions still form","Within 72 hours only, never after","Only after polymerase chain reaction confirms it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antivirals should be started as soon as possible, ideally within 72 hours of rash onset, and treatment is still given after 72 hours when new lesions are forming or there is ophthalmic involvement. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman is being counselled after a melanoma diagnosis and asks what most determines her outlook. What is the dominant prognostic variable?","choices":["Asymmetry","Lesion diameter","Breslow thickness","Anatomic subtype","Fitzpatrick skin type"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Survival drops sharply with increasing Breslow thickness and with nodal or distant spread. Breslow thickness is the dominant prognostic variable and must be accurately measured at the time of initial biopsy — which is why a shave biopsy that transects the base can compromise staging. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A student is seeing a 46-year-old woman with an undifferentiated complaint and is unsure how to begin working through it. What are the first three steps of the clinical reasoning process?","choices":["Order tests, interpret, then take a history","Gather, interpret, then form a problem representation","Generate hypotheses, gather, then test","Treat symptoms, observe, then diagnose","Apply a guideline, then confirm it fits"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The process begins with gathering initial patient information, then organising and interpreting it, then synthesising the information into a problem representation, before hypotheses are generated and tested. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has two round, sharply circumscribed, slightly erythematous scaly plaques on the forearm, each with an advancing scaly border and a clearer centre. Where should the specimen for potassium hydroxide microscopy be taken from?","choices":["A plucked hair from within the plaque","Normal skin adjacent to the lesion","The centre of the lesion, where the change began","The active advancing border of the lesion","A nail clipping from the same limb"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The potassium hydroxide specimen is taken from the active border, where the viable organism is concentrated. Culture is added for high clinical suspicion when the potassium hydroxide preparation is negative. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with a family history of melanoma has more than 100 naevi, several at least 5 mm with irregular, indistinct borders. How is a dysplastic naevus diagnosed, and what is the management?","choices":["Biopsy, excising where melanoma is a concern","Dermoscopy alone","Biopsy, then excise all naevi prophylactically","Clinical diagnosis; no biopsy needed","No follow-up once documented"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis of a dysplastic melanocytic nevus is by biopsy. Management includes observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where there is concern for melanoma, and sun protection. Family history is a risk factor, and dysplastic nevus syndrome may produce over 100 naevi by adolescence. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has moderate papulopustular and mixed acne of the face and upper back that has not responded to eight weeks of a topical antimicrobial. What regimen is recommended?","choices":["Oral isotretinoin as monotherapy","A topical retinoid and an oral antibiotic","An oral antibiotic alone, indefinitely","A topical retinoid alone","A topical retinoid, an oral antibiotic, and benzoyl peroxide"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Moderate papulopustular and mixed acne is treated with a topical retinoid and an oral antibiotic and topical benzoyl peroxide. Benzoyl peroxide is specifically added whenever an antibiotic is used, to reduce the risk of bacterial resistance. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
